{#- Shared Jinja helpers. It lives at the repository root, outside the
    ``_subdirectory`` copier renders, so it is an input that can never become
    output. Templates pull it in with an ``import`` tag on their first line.

    ``py_collection`` emits a Python tuple or list literal in the exact byte form
    ruff-format produces (ADR-030): one line when the whole statement fits in 88
    columns, otherwise one element per line with a trailing comma. The generated
    project sets ``skip-magic-trailing-comma``, so an always-exploded literal
    would be collapsed back and an always-inline one would be exploded -- either
    way the adopter's first ``prek run --all-files`` would rewrite the file. -#}
{%- macro py_collection(name, items, kind="tuple") -%}
{%- set _open = '(' if kind == 'tuple' else '[' -%}
{%- set _close = ')' if kind == 'tuple' else ']' -%}
{%- set _joined = items | map('tojson') | join(', ') -%}
{%- set _tail = ',' if kind == 'tuple' and items | length == 1 else '' -%}
{%- if (name ~ ' = ' ~ _open ~ _joined ~ _tail ~ _close) | length <= 88 -%}
{{ name }} = {{ _open }}{{ _joined }}{{ _tail }}{{ _close }}
{%- else -%}
{{ name }} = {{ _open }}
{%- for item in items %}
    {{ item | tojson }},
{%- endfor %}
{{ _close }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The agent-instruction prose. Rendered into ``AGENTS.md`` (and through it
    ``CLAUDE.md``) when ``include_ai_rulez`` is off, and into the ``.ai-rulez/``
    rule and context sources -- which ``ai-rulez generate`` fans out to every
    selected host -- when it is on (ADR-035). Both renderings are the same
    bytes because they call the same macro: a second copy of this prose, kept in
    sync by nothing, is exactly the drift the toggle exists to remove.

    Each macro emits a section *body* with no surrounding blank lines, so the
    caller owns the heading above it and the spacing around it. -#}
{%- macro agent_package_manager() -%}
- Use **uv**. Install dependencies with `uv sync`.
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro agent_commands() -%}
| Task | Command |
| ---- | ------- |
| Run one test file | `uv run --locked pytest tests/test_smoke.py -v` |
| Full test matrix | `uv run --locked tox run` |
| Lint + type-check | `uv run --locked tox run -e style` |
| Git hooks | `uv run --locked tox run -e prek` |
| Build package | `uv build` |
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro agent_external_references() -%}
| Need | File |
| ---- | ---- |
| Contributor setup & workflow | `.github/CONTRIBUTING.md` |
| Getting help | `.github/SUPPORT.md` |
| Security policy | `.github/SECURITY.md` |
| Changelog (owned by release-please) | `CHANGELOG.md` |
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ``components`` is ``interface_components`` (copier.yml): the interface
    packages in layering order. ``primary`` is ``primary_component`` and
    ``has_console_root`` is ``include_console_root`` -- passed in rather than
    read from the render context so the macro has one explicit contract, the
    same way ``py_collection`` takes its values. -#}
{%- macro agent_package_structure(pkg, components, has_console_root, primary) -%}
{%- set _row_label = {"web": "HTTP route or web handler", "gui": "GUI screen or widget", "tui": "TUI screen or widget", "mcp": "MCP tool or resource", "worker": "Message subscriber / broker handler"} -%}
**Do not create new top-level subpackages under `src/{{ pkg }}/`.**
New feature code goes inside an existing layer:

| What you are adding | Where it goes |
| ------------------- | ------------- |
{%- if primary %}
| Data every interface reports (version, runtime info, ...) | `core/app.py` |
{%- endif %}
| Business logic, domain models, app behavior | `core/` |
| External integrations (APIs, storage, brokers) | `core/` |
| Config / settings | `core/config.py` |
| Dependency-free helpers (no internal imports) | `utils/` |
{%- if has_console_root %}
| CLI command or subcommand | `cli/app.py` |
{%- endif %}
{%- for component in components %}
| {{ _row_label[component] }} | `{{ component }}/` |
{%- endfor %}

The layering is enforced in CI by import-linter (`[tool.importlinter]` in
`pyproject.toml`), whose contract is **exhaustive**: a subpackage not listed in
its `layers` fails `tox run -e style`. `core` may import `utils`; `utils` imports
nothing internal.
{%- if primary %}
`core/app.py` holds the version/runtime payload every interface renders; each
interface is an adapter over it and decides only how to transport, present, and
fail. Put a fact that more than one interface must report there, not in one of
them.
{%- endif %}
{%- if components | length > 1 %}
The interface packages ({{ components | join('/') }}) may import `core` but
are independent siblings — they may not import each other.
{%- elif components %}
`{{ components[0] }}` may import `core`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if has_console_root and components %}
`cli` sits above them as the orchestrator: it lazy-imports each one to launch it
as a subcommand.
{%- endif %}

Adding a top-level subpackage is an architecture change: propose it first, and if
agreed, add it to the import-linter contract in the same PR.
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro agent_conventions(pkg) -%}
- Source lives in `src/{{ pkg }}/`; tests mirror it under `tests/`.
- `src/{{ pkg }}/_version.py` is generated by hatch-vcs — never edit it.
- Absolute imports only; strict typing; max cyclomatic complexity 5 (ruff-enforced).
- Versions come from Git tags — do not hand-edit a version literal anywhere.
- Commit messages follow Conventional Commits; releases are automated by release-please.
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro agent_pull_requests() -%}
CI blocks a PR whose branch name or title breaks these conventions — follow them
before opening one:

- **Branch names** follow [Conventional Branch](https://conventionalbranch.org/):
  `<type>/<short-description>` (e.g. `feat/add-login`). Allowed types are
  `feature`/`feat`, `bugfix`/`fix`, `hotfix`, `release`, `chore`, plus the
  AI-agent prefixes `ai`, `copilot`, `cursor`, `claude`, `codex`. The description
  is lowercase alphanumerics separated by single `-` or `.` (no leading, trailing,
  or consecutive separators). Enforced by `check-branch-name.yml`, which also
  whitelists the `renovate/*` and `release-please--*` automation branches — leave
  those as-is.
- **PR titles** follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/)
  (e.g. `feat: add login`) — the title becomes the squash commit release-please
  parses, so it drives versioning. Enforced by `check-pr-title.yml`.
- Every human-authored PR must **link an issue** (a `Closes #N` keyword in the
  body) unless it carries the `no-issue` label. Enforced by
  `check-linked-issues.yml`, which auto-skips PRs opened by a bot account (login
  ending in `[bot]`) — don't fabricate an issue reference for those.
{%- endmacro -%}
